NNAAMMEE
       dumpe2fs - dump ext2/ext3/ext4 file system information

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       dduummppee22ffss  [  --bbffgghhiixxVV  ]  [  --oo  ssuuppeerrbblloocckk==_s_u_p_e_r_b_l_o_c_k  ]  [  --oo bblloocckk‐‐
       ssiizzee==_b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e ] _d_e_v_i_c_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       dduummppee22ffss prints the super block and blocks group  information  for  the
       file system present on _d_e_v_i_c_e_.

       NNoottee:: When used with a mounted file system, the printed information may
       be old or inconsistent.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --bb     print the blocks which are reserved as bad in the file system.

       --oo ssuuppeerrbblloocckk==_s_u_p_e_r_b_l_o_c_k
              use the block _s_u_p_e_r_b_l_o_c_k when examining the file  system.   This
              option  is not usually needed except by a file system wizard who
              is examining the remains of a very badly corrupted file system.

       --oo bblloocckkssiizzee==_b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e
              use blocks of _b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e bytes when examining  the  file  system.
              This option is not usually needed except by a file system wizard
              who is examining the remains of a very badly corrupted file sys‐
              tem.

       --ff     force  dumpe2fs to display a file system even though it may have
              some file system feature flags which dumpe2fs may not understand
              (and which can cause some of dumpe2fs's display to be suspect).

       --gg     display  the  group descriptor information in a machine readable
              colon-separated value format.   The  fields  displayed  are  the
              group  number;  the  number of the first block in the group; the
              superblock location (or -1 if not present); the range of  blocks
              used  by the group descriptors (or -1 if not present); the block
              bitmap location; the inode bitmap location;  and  the  range  of
              blocks used by the inode table.

       --hh     only display the superblock information and not any of the block
              group descriptor detail information.

       --ii     display the file system data from an image file created by ee22iimm‐‐
              aaggee, using _d_e_v_i_c_e as the pathname to the image file.

       --mm     If  the  mmmmpp feature is enabled on the file system, check if _d_e_‐
              _v_i_c_e is in use by another node, see ee22mmmmppssttaattuuss(8) for full  de‐
              tails.   If used together with the --ii option, only the MMP block
              information is printed.

       --xx     print the detailed group information block numbers in  hexadeci‐
              mal format

       --VV     print the version number of dduummppee22ffss and exit.

EEXXIITT CCOODDEE
       dduummppee22ffss exits with a return code of 0 if the operation completed with‐
       out errors.  It will exit with a non-zero return code if there are  any
       errors,  such as problems reading a valid superblock, bad checksums, or
       if the device is in use by another node and --mm is specified.

BBUUGGSS
       You may need to know the physical file system structure  to  understand
       the output.

